T1  - Long-term follow-up of patients with elevated IGF-1 and nadir GH > 0.4 microg/L but < 1 microg/L.
T3  - Comment in: Arch Endocrinol Metab. 2017 Oct;61(5):414-415<ovid:br/> PMID: 29166453 [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29166453]
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1590/2359-3997000000295
A1  - Rosario, Pedro Weslley
A1  - Calsolari, Maria Regina
Y1  - 2017//
Y2  - 20170918//
N1  - Comment in (CIN)
N2  - <ovid:b>OBJECTIVE</ovid:b>: To report the results of initial investigation and after 5 years of patients with a suspicious clinical scenario for acromegaly, elevated IGF-1, and nadir GH during an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) > 0.4 microg/L but < 1 microg/L., <ovid:b>SUBJECTS AND METHODS</ovid:b>: Seventeen patients who had elevated IGF-1 (outside puberty and pregnancy) in two measurements and GH between 0.4 and 1 microg/L during OGTT were selected., <ovid:b>RESULTS</ovid:b>: During initial assessment, only one patient had microadenoma on magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the pituitary. In this patient, IGF-1 returned to normal spontaneously after 5 years. In the remaining 16 patients, spontaneous normalization of IGF-1 was observed in four and IGF-1 continued to be elevated in 12 after 5 years. None of the latter patients developed a phenotype of acromegaly, changes in physiognomy or increase in IGF-1 and no tumor was detected by imaging methods. Two patients had nadir GH < 0.4 microg/L, while the nadir GH remained between 0.4 and 1 microg/L in 10 patients., <ovid:b>CONCLUSION</ovid:b>: In patients (notably young adult or adult women) without a typical phenotype in whom IGF-1 is measured due to a suspicious clinical scenario and is found to be slightly elevated, even if confirmed and in the absence of other causes, a nadir GH cut-off value of 0.4 microg/L instead of 1 microg/L in the OGTT might be inadequate for the diagnosis.
