T1  - Changes in weight and BMI following the diagnosis of type 1 diabetes in children and adolescents.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s00592-013-0524-4
A1  - de Vries, L
A1  - Bar-Niv, M
A1  - Lebenthal, Y
A1  - Tenenbaum, A
A1  - Shalitin, S
A1  - Lazar, L
A1  - Cohen, A
A1  - Phillip, M
Y1  - 2014//
Y2  - 20131025//
N2  - The aim of this study was to study weight and body mass index (BMI) before, at, and after diagnosis of type 1 diabetes (T1D) and to identify factors associated with weight gain. Studied retrospectively were 209 children <18 years with T1D followed for 6 years. Data collected included clinical and laboratory data before diagnosis, at diagnosis, and during 6 years of follow-up. Anthropometric parameters of patients were compared along follow-up and with those of their parents and siblings. Mean BMI-standard deviation score (SDS) was below average at diagnosis (-0.66 +/- 1.27), had increased to 0.37 +/- 0.93 at 3 months, and decreased to a nadir at 6 months in females and 12 months in males; between 1 and 3 years, there was a slight increase and between 3 and 6 years a further increase only in the females. BMI-SDS at 6 years was significantly higher than pre-diabetes BMI-SDS (0.35 +/- 0.83 vs. -0.04 +/- 1.23, p < 0.001). Patients' BMI-SDS at 6 years was similar to that of their parents and siblings, was higher in the females (0.53 +/- 0.74 vs. 0.27 +/- 0.82, p = 0.02) and in those keeping diabetes a secret (0.66 +/- 0.82 vs. 0.33 +/- 0.78, p = 0.027), and was not associated with age or pubertal stage at diagnosis, ethnicity, or metabolic control. A longer duration of insulin pump therapy was associated with a lower BMI-SDS (r = -0.2375, p < 0.025). BMI-SDS increased during the 6 years following diagnosis of T1D in pediatric patients, especially in the females, but remained in the normal range and was similar to that of other family members.
